{"package":"Indented.StubCommand","version":"1.4.0","assessment":"complete","packageSha256":"077c79e62c6a29a71b26abf1d4583188745bca512e91ab3da343d329665d4e98","scannedAt":"2026-10-24T22:54:07.277Z","scannerVersion":"0.2.0","rulesetVersion":"0.3.0","tools":{"powerShell":"7.6.8","psscriptAnalyzer":"1.25.0","psscriptAnalyzerExpected":"1.25.0","clamav":null},"packageUrl":"https://www.powershellgallery.com/packages/Indented.StubCommand/1.4.0","metadata":{"title":"Indented.StubCommand","description":"A stub command or module is intended for use with tools like Pester.\n\nA stub command is roughly similar to a mock, with the exclusion of the ability to fill the command body. It is designed to feed mock creation.\n\nWhen Pester to creates a mock the original command must be available. If a command or module is not available a function might be written to resemble the original command.\n\nThis module is used to decrease the work required to define a fabricated function by creating a stub from the original.\n\nStubs can be created for modules such as ServerManager so they appear to execute correctly from development clients. It allows modules like ActiveDirectory, Exchange, and WebAdministration to run from a build server without expicit module deployment or session imports.","authors":"Chris Dent","owners":"indented-automation","projectUrl":"https://github.com/indented-automation/Indented.StubCommand","publishedAt":"2019-08-13T08:35:03.773","updatedAt":"2026-10-24T10:00:01.497","tags":["Pester","PSModule","PSFunction_Get-StubRequiredType","PSCommand_Get-StubRequiredType","PSFunction_New-StubCommand","PSCommand_New-StubCommand","PSFunction_New-StubDynamicParam","PSCommand_New-StubDynamicParam","PSFunction_New-StubModule","PSCommand_New-StubModule","PSFunction_New-StubType","PSCommand_New-StubType","PSIncludes_Function"]},"dependencies":[],"binaryArtifacts":[],"fileInventory":[{"path":"_rels/.rels","size":514,"crc32":"dc094b0e","extension":"rels"},{"path":"Indented.StubCommand